{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>To the woman He said.</b> Nations asked Rabbi Yehoshua: why was Chava punished for eating from the tree of knowledge, since she had not yet been created at the time of the commandment? He said to them: concerning the man it is written upon all the limbs of the man, and she was created from his rib. Another interpretation: she included herself in the warning when she said, ״ומפרי העץ אשר בתוך הגן אמר אלקים לא תאכלו ממנו״." "<b>I will greatly multiply.</b> That you will live in sadness and toil all your days. And if you say: what do I care about the curse — I will bring in a maidservant to serve me? Scripture says <b>and your pregnancy</b> — meaning, you will conceive. Measure for measure: she filled her belly with the forbidden thing, therefore she is punished in her belly. And if you say: I will give birth with ease and tranquility? Scripture says בעצב תלדי, and that is the pain of childbirth. And if you say: I will guard myself from relations with my husband? Scripture says ואל אישך תשוקתך. And if you say: I will master my desire and not have relations with him at all? Scripture says <b>and he shall rule over you</b> — against your will you will have relations with him." "<b>And he shall rule over you.</b> Rashi explained: everything is from him and not from you. Meaning, even if she has no desire, he is able to have relations; but if he has no desire, she cannot. And he shall rule over you — our Rabbis expounded in Bereshit Rabbah: might one think this is dominion from every side, meaning that he can take her as a pledge? Scripture says לא יחבול רחים ורכב, and it refers to a woman, as written nearby לאלתר כי יקח איש אשה חדשה. And furthermore, this verse is translated in the Jerusalem Targum: לא תמשכנון רחיא ורכבא ארום צרכי נפשתא אינון, ולא תאסור כלין וחתנים ארום כל דעביד אלין כמאן דכפר בעלמא דאתי — meaning, if a man betroths a woman, he must not leave her a long time in betrothal."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The phrase לאלתר כי יקח איש אשה חדשה appears to cite Deuteronomy 24:5 in abbreviated, paraphrastic form; לאלתר (״immediately״) likely reflects the midrashic reading connecting the exemption from war to the present discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:02:50.906117141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}